# Graded Heterojunction Engineering for Hole-Conductor-Free Perovskite Solar Cells with High Hole Extraction Efficiency and Conductivity

## abstract
Despite great progress in the photovoltaic conversion efficiency (PCE) of inorganic-organic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs), the large-scale application of PSCs still faces serious challenges due to the poor-stability and high-cost of the spiro-OMeTAD hole transport layer (HTL). It is of great fundamental importance to rationally address the issues of hole extraction and transfer arising from HTL-free PSCs. Herein, a brand-new PSC architecture is designed by introducing multigraded-heterojunction (GHJ) inorganic perovskite CsPbBrx I3-x layers as an efficient HTL. The grade adjustment can be achieved by precisely tuning the halide proportion and distribution in the CsPbBrx I3-x film to reach an optimal energy alignment of the valance and conduction band between MAPbI3 and CsPbBrx I3-x . The CsPbBrx I3-x GHJ as an efficient HTL can induce an electric field where a valance/conduction band edge is leveraged to bend at the heterojunction interface, boosting the interfacial electron-hole splitting and photoelectron extraction. The GHJ architecture enhances the hole extraction and conduction efficiency from the MAPbI3 to the counter electrode, decreases the recombination loss during the hole transfer, and benefits in increasing the open-circuit voltage. The optimized HTL-free PCS based on the GHJ architecture demonstrates an outstanding thermal stability and a significantly improved PCE of 11.33%, nearly 40% increase compared with 8.16% for pure HTL-free devices.

Despite great progress in the photovoltaic conversion efficiency (PCE) of inorganic–organic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs), the large-scale application of PSCs still faces serious challenges due to the poor-stability and high-cost of the spiro-OMeTAD hole transport layer (HTL). It is of great fundamental importance to rationally address the issues of hole extraction and transfer arising from HTL-free PSCs. Herein, a brand-new PSC architecture is designed by introducing multigraded-heterojunction (GHJ) inorganic perov­skite $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}|_{3-x}$ layers as an efficient HTL. The grade adjustment can be achieved by precisely tuning the halide proportion and distribution in the $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}|_{3-x}$ film to reach an optimal energy alignment of the valance and conduction band between $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ and $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x^{1}3-x^{.}}$ The $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}\mathsf{I}_{3-x}\mathsf{C H})$ as an efficient HTL can induce an electric field where a valance/conduction band edge is leveraged to bend at the heterojunction interface, boosting the interfacial electron–hole splitting and photoelectron extraction. The GHJ architecture enhances the hole extraction and conduction efficiency from the $M A P b\mathsf{I}_{3}$ to the counter electrode, decreases the recombination loss during the hole transfer, and benefits in increasing the open-circuit voltage. The optimized HTL-free PCS based on the GHJ architecture demonstrates an outstanding thermal stability and a significantly improved PCE of $11.33\%$ , nearly $40\%$ increase compared with $8.16\%$ for pure HTL-free devices.
Hybrid organic–inorganic halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have shown a promising future with the merits of high photo­ voltaic performance and low-cost fabrication techniques.[1–5] A typical PSC consists of an electron transport layer (e.g., $\mathrm{TiO}_{2})$ ), a perovskite optical absorption layer (e.g., $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3})$ , a hole transport layer (HTL) (e.g., spiro-OMeTAD), and a metal contact electrode.[2] Dispiritingly, the high-cost and low-con­ ductivity of conventional HTL of spiro-OMeTAD hinders the large-scale application and performance instability of PSCs.[6] To solve these obstacles, a great deal of attention has been paid on the investigation of novel HTLs, such as organic materials of poly(triarylamine) and poly(ethylenedioxythiophene):polystyrene­ sulfonate (PEDOT:PSS),[7,8] even graphene and single-walled carbon nanotube.[9,10]
It is found that HTL-free PSCs could also operate efficiently since the unique ambipolar property of the perovskite allows it to serve not only as a light har­ vester but also as a hole conductor.[11] Recently, Etgar and Laban first reported depleted HTL-free lead halide iodide het­ erojunction solar cells, achieving a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $8\%$ .[12] Han and co-workers fabricated HTL-free fully printable mesoscopic carbon-based PSCs with enhanced efficiency and sta­ bility.[13,14] However, the photovoltaic con­ version efficiency of HTL-free PSCs is still lower, which might be attributed to high electron–hole recombination rate derived from the poor hole extraction efficiency and low transfer rate from the perovskite light absorption layer.[15] It is challenging and of greatly fundamental importance to rationally offset the issues arising from HTL-free for improving the photovoltaic performance of PSCs.[16] Further device structural designs and investigations are essential to offset the issues arising from HTL-free for improving the photovoltaic performance.
It is worth noting that, heterojunction engineering, based on exquisitely tailoring and optimizing of energy-band align­ ment, has been demonstrated effectively in improving the photovoltaic performance in a number of PV devices. Theoreti­ cally, the formation of composite heterostructure semiconduc­ tors can introduce an electric field where a valance/conduction band edge is leveraged to bend at the heterojunction interface, boosting the interfacial electron–hole splitting and photoelec­ tron extraction. It is reported that constructing an interfacial layer with suitable energy-level can effectively facilitate carrier injection and obstructs the charge carrier flowing back, bene­ ficial to the reduction of recombination loss.[17,18] In addition, several studies conduct the concept of planar-heterojunction (PHJ) and bulk-heterojunction (BHJ) into PSCs by blending perovskite with fullerene, enhancing short-circuit current den­ sities $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ and fill factors (FF) as a result of the balance in the charge carrier extraction efficiency and the enlarged interfacial area between the perovskite materials and the fullerene deriva­ tives.[19–21] However, one remained obstacle in the heterojunc­ tion architecture is the relatively shallow depletion layer depth, resulting in electron/hole accumulation in the undepleted region. It has been proposed that energy structure grading can help charge transfer by extending the width of depleted layer in which a significant electrical field is provided for drift-based driving force.[22,23] Constructing controllable grade heterojunc­ tion with precise gradient distribution, convertible energy-level depth, and desired energy-level alignment remains a challenge for high-performance perovskite solar cells.
Recently, pure inorganic perovskite $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ nanocrystals (NCs) have drawn substantial attention for the high photo­ luminescence quantum yield, narrow line width, and high stability.[24,25] Especially, perovskite $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}\mathrm{NCs}$ provide the experimentally convenient route to build an electron/hole funnel and achieve the energy-level alignment by tuning the proportion of halide (X).[26] Additionally, as the optical absorp­ tion layer used in perovskite solar cells, the cubic- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}\mathrm{NC}$ films exhibit an outstanding photoelectric and photophysical properties, which enhances the PCE to over $10\%$ for the pure inorganic perovskite solar cells.[27] The charge carriers of these NCs are highly mobile $(\approx4500\ c m^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ with long diffusion lengths $(>9.2~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ , which is orders of magnitude higher com­ pared to hybrid halide perovskite films and single crystalline semiconductors.[28]
Here, we design a brand-new PSC architecture with a $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}}\mathrm{\cdotCsPbBr_{\itx}I_{3-x}}$ perovskite graded heterojunction (GHJ) by introducing $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}\mathrm{NCs}$ on the top of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . The grade adjustment can be achieved by precisely tuning halide proportion and distribution in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ film to reach an optimal energy alignment of valance and conduction band between $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}.$ We demonstrate that a rational graded heterojunction architecture can enhances the hole extraction and conduction efficiency from $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ to counter electrode, decreases the recombination loss during the hole transfer, and benefits in increasing the open-circuit voltage. The optimized HTL-free PCS based on GHJ architec­ ture demonstrates an outstanding thermal-stability and a sig­ nificantly improved PCE of $11.33\%$ , nearly $40\%$ increase com­ pared with pure HTL-free devices $(8.16\%)$ . This design of con­ trollable gradient heterojunction offers a new way to solve the electron or hole extraction obstacle not only in HTL-free PSCs, but also in other structural systems of PSCs.
With the benefit of wavelength tunability via composition and similar energy structure with organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite, $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}\mathrm{NCs}$ can be the suitable candidate for fabricating heterojunction with $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ . For this purpose, the proportion of $\mathrm{Br/I}$ in $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}\mathrm{NCs}$ are fine-tuned with the nanocrystal size fixed. As confirmed by X-ray diffraction of NCs (Figure 1a), the crystal structures of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{0.7}\mathrm{I}_{2.3}$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{0.3}\mathrm{I}_{2.7}$ , and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NCs are all assigned as the cubic structure (JCPDS 54-0752). As increasing bromide incorpora­ tion, the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ shift gradually to high-angle position, demonstrating the decrease of lattice con­ stant. In Figure 1b, the visible-light absorption and emission spectra could be finely tuned via compensational modulation. A linear blueshift of photometric characteristics from 670 to $620~\mathrm{nm}$ could be achieved by gradually increasing the propor­ tion of bromide to $33\%$ in the colloidal $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}\mathrm{NCs}$ .
In order to assess the effect of $\mathrm{Br/I}$ proportion on the opto­ electronic properties in the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC films, ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) is performed on NC film samples with different bromide contents. The full UPS spectra and the energy differences are shown in Figure 2a, from which both Fermi level $\left(E_{\mathrm{f}}\right)$ and VB with respect to vacuum level can be derived. Figure 2b shows the enlarged view of secondary electron cut-off region where the $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ is determined by the dif­ ference between the incident photon energy $(21.2\ \mathrm{\eV})$ and the binding energy of the secondary electron cut-off. With the increase of bromide content, the secondary cut-off shifts to higher binding energy, demonstrating the substitution of bro­ mide for iodide can reduce the Fermi level of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ The difference between $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{V}}$ is determined by the intersection of linear portion of spectra near the Fermi edge (low binding energy region) with the baseline as depicted in Figure 2c. The valence band maximum (VBM) edges for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{0.3}\mathrm{I}_{2.7}$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{0.7}\mathrm{I}_{2.3}$ , and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ are located at 5.35, 5.30, 5.20, and $5.15\ \mathrm{eV}$ (with respect to vacuum level), respectively. Notably, all the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ $(x=0-1$ ) possess higher VBM than $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ $(5.39\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and the values of VBM elevate gradually with the increasing of bromide content, satisfying the requirement of energy structure alignment. However, if further increasing the proportion of bromide, the VBM presents an opposite trend, below the VBM of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , as shown in Figure S8 (Supporting Information). The Tauc plots in Figure 2d are depicted to char­ acterize the bandgap between VB and CB edges of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC films. Similar with $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ and $\begin{array}{r}{E_{\mathrm{V}},}\end{array}$ the bandgap exhibits a slight widening of ${\approx}0.1$ eV for each composition as shown in Figure 2e.
To examine the effect of heterojunction type (type I or type II) on the photovoltaic performance, the perovskite solar cells based on single heterojunction of $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}}\mathrm{-CsPbBr_{\itx}I_{3-x}}(x=0\mathrm{-}3)$ are first fabricated and the corresponding photovoltaic data are displayed in Figure S9 and Table S1 (Supporting Informa­ tion).The devices based on $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ which have deeper VB than $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ exhibit enhanced PCE and boosted hole extraction rate compared with the device in the absence of $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}\mathrm{NC}$ film. Oppositely, the type I heterojunction ones for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{2}\mathrm{I}$ with shallower VB present decline tendency for both PCE and hole extraction rate.
We then fabricate perovskite solar cells based on the graded heterojunction structure in which we deposit different compo­ nents of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC films orderly on the top of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ based on different halide concentration, keeping the total thick­ ness fixed. Figure 3a shows the diagram of energy-level align­ ment for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{0.3}\mathrm{I}_{2.7},$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{0.7}\mathrm{I}_{2.3}$ , and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ . For 1-grade, single $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ NC film is coupled with $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , forming type II heterojunction. The 2-grade and 3-grade cor­ respond to $\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}/C s P b B r_{0.3}I_{2.7}}$ and $\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}/C s P b B r_{0.3}I_{2.7}/}$ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{0.7}\mathrm{I}_{2.3}.$ , respectively. The 4-grade is 4 layers heterojunc­ tion consisting of $\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}/C s P b B r_{0.3}I_{2.7}/C s P b B r_{0.7}I_{2.3}/C s P b B r I_{2}}$ , just as depicted in energy level diagram (Figure 3a). Figure 3b depicts the variation of energy level sat the grade heterojunction interface where Fermi levels balance at the same level due to the electron and hole flow in the opposite direction reaching to lowest CB and highest VB, respectively.[29] The grade het­ erojunction results in a reduction of the work function and a downward vacuum level shift at the interface. Moreover, the difference between the Fermi level and the valence band edge decreases with increasing Br substitution amount.
The energy level alignment and variation demonstrate the role of grade heterojunction as a hole extraction and conduction layer between the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ and counter electrode. Figure 3c,d,f show the schematic of the device structures studied in this work. In HTL-free device, excited electrons flow from $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ not only to electron extraction layer, but also to counter elec­ trode, which is in the opposite direction for the photocurrent, and might cause serious interfacial electron–hole recombina­ tion (Figure 3c). By introducing $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ forming heterojunction (Figure 3d), an additional driving force for holes is generated at the heterojunction interface owing to the aid of built-in elec­ tric field of valance band offset. Moreover, the conduction band offset between the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ layers offers an energy barrier that restrains photogenerated electron from flowing to counter electrode (Figure 3d). For further increasing the grade to 2-, 3-, and 4-grade (Figure 3e), the multigrade type II hetero­ junction where both VB and CB edge array gradiently creates a “hole channel,” deriving hole conduction to counter electrode, efficiently, and provides more energy barriers that prevent the generation of reverse photocurrent inside the device.
For examining the effect of graded heterojunction pro­ posed above on the performance of HTL-free devices, we con­ duct the photoelectric measurements. Figure 4a,b shows the schematic of device structure and cross-section SEM image for the corresponding one. The optimized gradient $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC film (containing of $\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}/C s P b B r_{0.3}I_{2.7}/C s P b B r_{0.7}I_{2.3})}$ with around $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ is deposited on optical absorption layer $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer acts as electron extraction layer and $\mathrm{Ag}$ as counter electrode. Figure 4c shows the typical $J{-}V$ characteristics of PSCs based on HTL-free and grade $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC films addition, and corresponding photovol­ taic parameters are summarized in Table 1. The HTL-free PSC (0-grade) exhibits an FF of $56.3\%$ , a short-circuit photo­ current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $15.92\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , an open-circuit photo­ voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $0.91\mathrm{~V~}$ and a corresponding PCE of $8.16\%$ , which is similar with the previous work reported by Laban and Etgar.[12] After adding single $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ film, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and PCE show the increase to $17.40\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and 8. $63\%$ , while FF and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ decrease to $55.7\%$ and $0.89~\mathrm{V},$ respectively. With the increase of the grade from 1- to 3-grade, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ maintains an increasing trend from 17.40 to $19.21\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ corre­ sponding with FF, is enhanced gradually from 0.89 to $0.95{\mathrm{~V}},$ and from $55.7\%$ to $62.1\%$ , respectively. The champion photo­ voltaic conversion efficiency can reach $11.33\%$ for 3-grade, which is $40\%$ enhancement compared to that of pure HTLfree PSC. However, with increasing the grade to 4-grade, the FF and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ present a slight decline $56.8\%$ and $0.90\mathrm{~V})$ ), and the PCE decreases to $9.25\%$ .
The corresponding incident monochromatic photon-toelectron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra are depicted in Figure 4d. IPCE is defined as the number of generated elec­ trons divided by the amount of incident photons. The IPCE spectra for all grades cover the same visible wavelength region, indicating that the variation of grade basically has no effect on the absorption range. However, the intensity of photore­ sponse in the entire spectra region exhibits a significant differ­ ence with the grade change, specifically in the region near the band edge. Clearly, with the increase of grade, the intensity of IPCE increases at first and decreases subsequently, displaying a similar trend to that of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . Due to the controlled thickness of different grades, the enhancement of IPCE is mostly attri­ buted to an electronic improvement, but not enhanced light harvesting.[30]
To understand the interfacial photoexcited carrier extraction efficiency, we perform the time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) characterization (Figure 4e) for a semidevice containing of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC film and $\mathrm{Ag}$ film. By simply fabricating solid thin films in the presence or absence of an exciton-quenching layer, and modeling the PL decay to a diffu­ sion equation, it is possible to accurately determine the exaction lifetime and diffusion rate.[11] Moreover, we eliminate the effect of electron extraction layer by just spin-coating $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ on the glass. The HTL-free sample (ungrade) exhibits a PL decay lifetime of 17.54 ns. For the 1-grade case, the lifetime decreases slightly to 16.43 ns. Since there is no electron extraction layer in the sample, the PL decay is attributed to carrier extraction by $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC layer. Keeping the thickness fixed, increasing the grade to 2-grade and 3-grade boost the quenching rate (9.23 and 7.21 ns), significantly, implying that holes separated from photogenerated excitons within the perovskite layer are more efficiently extracted from $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ to counter electrode.[31] Whereas, for the 4-grade, the PL lifetime increases to 17.21 ns. The opposite trend might be attributed to the emergence of a platform of energy-level due to the excessive refinement of $\mathrm{Br/I}$ ratio, which reduces the driving force for carrier and the extrac­ tion kinetic.
Electrochemical impedance spectros­ copy (EIS) measurements are performed to investigate the interfacial reaction of photo­ excited carriers and the charge transport kinetics of PSCs based in the presence of different grades as depicted in Figure 4f. In normal PSCs with a structure of EEL/ perovskite/HTL/BE, there are mainly three RC elements corresponding to three interface of EEL/perovskite, EEL/HTL, and
HTL/BE, respectively.[32] Here, the perovskite itself acts both light absorber layer and hole transport layer, thus, only two RC elements (EEL/perovskite and perovskite/BE) appears, as shown in Figure 4f. The first circle at high frequency in the inset of Figure $4\mathrm{f}$ represents the impedance at perovskite/BE interface, where the smaller semicircle represents smaller transporta­ tion and exchange resistance.[14] The high frequency resistance values of PSCs based on 0-grade, 1-grade, 2-grade, 3-grade, and 4-grade calculated according to the equivalent circuit cor­ respond to 182.4,94.3, 26.4, 17.8, and $48.5\ \Omega$ , respectively. It is clearly seen that the value of impedance in the presence of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC film addition exhibits significant decrease compared to 0-grade one, implying an efficient hole transport and carrier exchange at the interface between $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and counter electrode.[14] Furthermore, with the increase of grade to 3-grade, the resistance value exhibits a decline trend, demon­ strating faster hole conduction and less electron–hole recom­ bination loss between $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and counter electrode.[14,33,34] The resistance for 4-grade shows the similar abnormal trend as TRPL, which means the inefficient hole transport for excessive refinement of grade.
In order to better understand the photovoltaic performance enhancement mechanism of PSCs including the $J_{\mathrm{sc}},\quad V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ FF, and PCE, the corresponding issues are discussed as fol­ lowing. For the enhancement of the $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ two elucidations can be accounted. The improvement of hole collection efficiency, on the one hand, plays a key role in the enhancement of photo­ current density. By properly introducing grade $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC films with energy level (both VB and CB) alignment, holes can be efficiently extracted and transferred at the aid of built-in elec­ tric field, as a result of which, the photocurrent can be enhanced significantly. On the other hand, the potential barriers arising from type II heterojunction block the reverse transfer of excited electrons, which suppress the electron–hole recombination and decreases the loss of photocurrent. Moreover, the increase of grade of GHJ provides more carriers for recombination of reverse electrons with holes, and thus explains the reason for a gradual increase of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ .
The evolution of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF with elevated grade exhibits similar trend, which can be explained as follows. The $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ value in PCSs is empirically determined by Equation (1):
$$
V_{\mathrm{oc}}\approx(1/e)(\left|E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}^{\mathrm{donor}}\right|-\left|E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}^{\mathrm{acceptor}}\right|)
$$
where $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}^{\mathrm{donor}}$ is the HOMO level of the donor and $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}^{\mathrm{acceptor}}$ is the LUMO level of the acceptor.[35] In HTL-free PSCs systems, the depleted heterojunction structure in which $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}^{\mathrm{donor}}$ and ELacUceMptOor equates, respectively, to the Fermi level of p-type and n-type semiconductor can be used to interpret the variation of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ [12,36] At equilibrium, the Fermi level in the whole sample must align. Therefore, the gradiently widen bandgap for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ with increased bromide proportion would amplify the difference of Fermi level for acceptor and donor, resulting in the improvement of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . For FF, the enhancement can be accounted for the sup­ pression of interfacial electron–hole recombination owing to the multipotential barriers provided by grade heterojunction.[17] How­ ever, for the abnormal change in 1-grade and 4-grade in both $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF, we attribute this decrease to the relatively short minority carrier diffusion length $(\mathrm{W_{d}})$ of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NCs, which causes an un-depleted region as depicted in Figure 3d.[22] The photovol­ taic performance measurement with respect to $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC film thickness gives more intuitive interpretation as shown in the Supporting Information. As a consequence, $J_{\mathrm{sc}},V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ and FF all are enhanced, resulting in a significant improvement in the PCE.
The thermal- and humidity-stability tests for the bestperformance grade heterojunction PSCs are carried out in comparison with the devices based on conventional HTL (spiro-OMeTAD) and without HTL without encapsulation. For the thermal-stability measurements shown in Figure 5a,b, the effect of high temperature on the photovoltaic performance of different solar cells is revealed. With the temperature increasing (Figure 5a), the efficiency of PSCs based on spiro HTL shows a sharp decline to only $40\%$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ while the efficiency of PCSs based on grade $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}\mathrm{NC}$ film exhibits more stable to $75\%$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ high temperature. Similarly, at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ working environment, the PSCs devices based on spiro HTL see the fast decrease to $20\%$ just within $200\mathrm{{h}}$ . The samples in the absence of spiro HTL show a parallel decay rate. The PSCs based on grade $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}\mathrm{NC}$ film possess the most stable photoelectric performance of $70\%$ after $200\mathrm{{h}}$ at a temperature of $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ It is shown that the efficiency of devices in the presence of conven­ tional HTL (spiro-OMeTAD) decreases sharply under the high temperature, which might be the result of the decomposition of organic components and the poor conducting of doped ions.[10] The outstanding thermal-stability of PSCs based on GHJ can be owing to the superior thermal-stability for pure inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ itself which operates efficiently at high tempera­ ture, as well as the compact between $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}$ NC film and $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ . In addition, the humidity stability is also studied by keeping the devices at $40\%$ humidity atmosphere (Figure 5c). The efficiency of devices without HTL decreases to $30\%$ within $200\mathrm{~h~}$ due to the rapid decomposition of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ . Compared with PSCs in the presence and absence of spiro-OMeTAD HTL, the PSCs based on HTL of GHJ $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ heterojunction, the PCE performance stability shows a significant enhancement because of the protection and blocking effect for obstructing the contact of atmosphere and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , which slows down the decomposition of organic–inorganic perovskite to some extent.
In summary, high-performance and stable HTL-free pero­ vskite solar cells based on $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}}–C s P b B r_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3-x}\ \mathrm{GHJ}$ architec­ ture is fabricated and characterized for the first time, which achieves a significantly enhanced PCE of $11.33\%$ (nearly $40\%$ increase compared with $8.16\%$ for pure HTL-free devices). The improved photovoltaic performance originates from three advantages. The grade heterojunction presented demonstrates an enhancement in hole extraction and conduction from light absorption layer to the counter electrode, which is the first and foremost advantage for this new structure. The second advan­ tage is that this GHJ architecture is effective to reduce the inter­ facial recombination loss by multigrade potential barriers. The third one can be assigned to the increase of open circuit voltage without modifying the light absorption layer. This work high lights the potential to improve the efficiency through a GHJ architecture strategy and offers a new way to solve the electron or hole extraction obstacle not only in HTL-free PSCs, but also in other structural systems of PSCs.
## Experimental Section
Material Synthesis: The colloidal $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}|_{3-x}$ nanocrystals were synthesized as following.[23] Briefly, Cs-oleate solution was prepared at first by mixing ${\mathsf{C s}}_{2}{\mathsf{C O}}_{3}$ $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{0.8\g}}$ Aladdin, $99.9\%$ ), 1-octadecene (ODE, $30~\mathsf{m L}$ , Aladdin, $90\%$ ), and oleic acid (OA, $2.5~\mathsf{m L}$ , Aladdin, $90\%$ ) into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ 3-neck flask and heating to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to obtain a clear solution under ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ . Next, The $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}|_{3-x}$ (taking $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ for example here) NC precursor was prepared by adding ODE $(5~\mathsf{m L})$ and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $87~\mathsf{m g}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ Aladdin, $99.9\%$ into $25~\mathrm{mL}$ 3-neck flask, drying under vacuum at ${\mathsf{l}}20{\mathsf{\textperthousand}}$ for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ , and then injecting $0.5~\mathsf{m L}$ dried OA and $0.5~\mathsf{m L}$ dried oleylamine (OLA, Aladdin, $80-90\%$ ) into this flask under the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ at this temperature to dissolve the $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ . After the solution became clear, a suitable temperature $(770~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ was set and $0.4~\mathsf{m L}$ prepared Cs-oleate solution was injected quickly. After $\textsf{5s}$ , the obtained solution was put into ice bath to quench the reaction. The NCs were precipitated by centrifugation at 3000 rpm for 1 min and 12 000 rpm for $5\mathrm{\min}$ with the moderate amount of MeOAc (Aladdin, $99.5\%\$ ) to assist the precipitation. The acquired precipitate was redispersed in the octane (Aladdin, $99\%$ ) at a concentration of around $50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . For other $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}\mathsf{I}_{3-x}\mathsf{N C s}$ , the addition amount of $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ (Aladdin, $99.999\%$ ) and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ depended on the stoichiometric ratio of $\mathsf{B r}/|$ in $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}|_{3-x}.$
Device Preparation: Fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO, Opvtech) glass was etched first by Zn powder and diluted HCl $(2~\mathsf{M})$ solution to obtain desirable pattern. Then, etched FTO glass was cleaned by acetone and ethanol, followed by oxygen plasma treatment for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . Afterward, a thin $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ compact layer and mesoporous layer were prepared separately by spin-coating $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ precursor solution and $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ paste at 2500 rpm for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ followed by sintering at $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ and $550~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , respectively. The $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ precursor solution was synthesized by dissolving methylammonium iodide $(C H_{3}N H_{3}l)$ and lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2})$ in N,N-dimethylformamide (Aladdin, $99\%$ ) at 1:1 molar ratio, and then the precursor solution was spin-coated on the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer at 1000 rpm (10 s) and 5000 rpm (20 s) with dropping of toluene at the second spincoating to control surface coverage, then annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10\min$ . The preparation of $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}|_{3-x}$ films consists of three stages as depicted in Figure S3 (Supporting Information). First, the $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{x}|_{3-x}$ NC solution is spread over the surface of $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ film, then spin-coating to a desirable speed to evaporate the solvent. Second, the ligand solution $({\mathsf{P b}}({\mathsf{O A c}})_{2}$ (Aladdin, $99\%$ in anhydrous MeOAc is dropped on the NC film for several seconds followed by high-speed spin-coating. Third, the film is washed with neat MeOAc solvent by spin-coating. The procedure is repeated several times to build desirable thick films. Finally, an around $50\ \mathsf{n m\ M o O_{3}}$ (Aladdin, $99.9\%$ ) layer and a $50\ \mathsf{n m\ A g}$ counter electrode were prepared by thermal evaporation.
Characterization: The composition and structure of obtained $\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ NCs were characterized by X-ray diffractometer (Philips Rigaku D/Max-kA X-ray diffractometer) and high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM, Phillips Tecnai 20U-Twin). The morphology of $\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ NC film and $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ film were characterized via field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, SU-70). The optical absorption and emission spectra were collected using a TU-1901 spectro-photometer and a Fluorescent transmitter (Hitachi, F4600), respectively. The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra (NEXUS 670) were used to characterize the surface functional group of NC film. The energy levels of $\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ NC films were recorded by ultraviolet photoelectron spectrum (AXIS ULTRA DLD). The photoelectric data of current–voltage curves of solar devices were acquired via a solar simulator (Newport, Class 3A, 94023A) with a Keithley 2420 under simulated AM $\rceil.5\textrm{C}$ sunlight $(700\ m\times)\ c m^{-2})$ . The solar cells measured were masked with a metal aperture to define the active area to be around $0.09\ c m^{2}$ . And a power source (Newport 300  W xenon lamp, 66920) equipped with a monochromator (Newport Cornerstone 260) and a multimeter (Keithley 2400) was used to record the IPCE. The time-resolved photoluminescence measurement were carried by FLS920 all functional fluorescence spectrometer (Edinburgh). The EIS was carried out under 1 sun light using a Princeton Parstate 2273A.
The stability measurements were performed based on the decay percentage of power conversion efficiency of perovskite solar cells for conversional HTL spiro-OMeTAD, HTL-free and 3-grade $\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ NCs film, respectively. The experiments were lasted for $200\mathrm{~h~}$ with a test every $\rceil0\mathrm{~h~}$ . For the Normalized PCE-Temperature measurement, a hot plate was applied to regulate the temperature from 20 to $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under the condition of isolation from atmosphere. For the measurement of thermal-stability as a function of times, the solar cells were kept in glovebox for excluding the effect of other conditions. The humiditystability characteristics were conducted electric moister buster cabinet with a settled humidity of $40\%$ at RT.
